<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.8.4: Writing More Inequalities</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00492</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.8.4: Writing More Inequalities</md:title>
    <md:uuid>702adee3-cd2c-4747-a702-1a1cc18f27e1</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Han has a budget of $25 to buy grapes. Write inequalities to represent the number of pounds of grapes that Han could buy in each situation:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Grapes cost $1.99 per pound.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( 1.99g ≤ 25 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Grapes cost $2.49 per pound.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( 2.49g ≤ 25 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Grapes cost $\(c\) per pound.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( cg ≤ 25 \)</para>

<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.
</para>
</section>
</content>
